# Accepted vulnerability findings for the Python 3.14 build unit, scoped to this family and nothing
# else. Both entries are reported from the CycloneDX BOM that pip 26.2 ships at
# pip/_vendor/bom.cdx.json, which lists pip's private vendored dependencies.
#
# They are accepted only on the targets that must carry pip (builder, development, cli). The
# production runtime target uninstalls pip entirely and is scanned with no ignore file, so a change
# that puts an installer back into production fails the gate instead of inheriting these entries.
#
# Note that pip 26.1.2, the version the upstream python image ships, vendors the very same two
# versions but ships no BOM, so it scans clean while carrying identical code. Pinning pip forward to
# 26.2 is deliberate: the risk is the same either way and this way it is disclosed here rather than
# hidden by a scanner blind spot.
#
# Accepted 2026-08-11. Revisit when a pip release older than the 7-day dependency cooldown vendors
# msgpack >= 1.2.1 and declares setuptools >= 78.1.1. Drop the entry and bump PIP_VERSION.

# msgpack 1.1.2, out-of-bounds read and crash on Unpacker reuse (GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964). Present on
# disk at pip/_vendor/msgpack, where it backs the CacheControl HTTP cache of pip. Every image in
# this repository sets PIP_NO_CACHE_DIR=1, so that cache is off and the decoder is never fed remote
# bytes. There is no pip release that vendors the fixed 1.2.1.
GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964

# setuptools 70.3.0, path traversal in PackageIndex (CVE-2025-47273). Declared in pip's vendor
# manifest, but the setuptools package is not actually on disk: pip vendors only the pkg_resources
# subpackage, and package_index.py, the file the advisory covers, is absent from the image. The
# finding is a manifest artifact with no vulnerable code behind it.
CVE-2025-47273
